  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "v4.2.4 - Recover from a cleared OPFS cache instead of throwing. OPFS is not ours alone: the browser evicts origin storage under pressure, the user can clear site data, and any other holder of the origin can remove the directory - and a torrent restored from that cache then holds a bitfield that is simply wrong. Until now the only symptom was an unrecoverable InvalidOperationException from the first read. A read that finds the bitfield claiming a piece the store cannot serve now clears the bit, re-arms the piece, resumes if paused and re-fetches it (at most once per piece per read, so a store that genuinely cannot hold it still fails loudly), and it re-persists the .torrent because the pieces are not the whole cache entry. PersistStateAsync no longer leaves a half entry - a state.json without its .torrent cannot be restored. Both piece-completion paths now write to the store BEFORE setting the bitfield bit unconditionally; the zero-copy path wrote only when the store was an AsyncFSChunkStore and set the bit regardless. AsyncFSChunkStore reports a PiecesWritten count and checks DirectoryExists before listing, so \"no file\" can finally distinguish \"never written\" from \"written and lost\".",
